rkloads in response to event-based triggers such as Azure Queue Storage mes=
sages =E2=80=94 exactly matching the behavior of Azure Functions hosted in =
the Consumption plan. KEDA acts as a metrics adapter, feeding external even=
t metrics into Kubernetes' HPA to drive autoscaling decisions.</p><p><br></=
p><p><code><strong>Install Kubernetes-based Event Driven Autoscaling (KEDA)=
</strong></code> is <strong>correct</strong> because KEDA enables event-dri=
ven scaling, including the ability to scale from zero =E2=80=94 which repli=
cates the serverless, on-demand model used in the Azure Functions Consumpti=
on plan. KEDA supports native integration with Azure Queue Storage triggers=
, which is a key requirement for App1. It works by monitoring queue length =
and exposing that metric to HPA, allowing Kubernetes to adjust pod count ac=
cordingly.</p><p><br></p><p><code>Enable and configure the virtual node add=
-on</code> is <strong>incorrect</strong> because while it allows AKS to bur=
st into Azure Container Instances (ACI) for additional capacity, it does no=
t support event-driven autoscaling. It is a compute scaling feature, not an=
 event-based trigger mechanism.</p><p><br></p><p><code><strong>Install Virt=
ual Kubelet</strong></code> is <strong>incorrect</strong> because it is the=
 underlying open-source component used by the virtual node add-on, but it i=
s not recommended for direct use in AKS. Instead, Azure provides the manage=
d virtual node add-on as the preferred and supported integration.</p><p><br=
></p><p><code>Enable autoscaling at the AKS cluster level</code> is <strong=
